##:7:NTexUp## - Declarative Document & Presentation Generator

```sh NTexUp``` is an open-source, text-based document and presentation generator designed to make the creation of professional, math-heavy, and reusable teaching materials effortless.

##) INSTALLATION

```sh
nuts install ntexup
```

##) QUICK START

```sh
# Create a new project (interactive)
nuts ntexup new myproject
cd myproject

# Open project in the viewer (uses current directory)
nuts ntexup show

# Generate a PDF from current directory
nuts ntexup pdf
```

---

##) COMMAND REFERENCE

###) Document Viewing & Editing

####) ```sh show | open [path]```
Open a document or project in the GUI viewer. If no path is specified, opens the current directory.

```sh
# Open current directory (most common usage)
cd myproject
nuts ntexup show

# Open a specific document
nuts ntexup show document.ntx

# Open a project from another location
nuts ntexup show ./myproject

# Open with GUI explicitly enabled
nuts ntexup show --gui
```

When opening a directory, NTexUp automatically detects the project structure and loads all pages from the project's main file.


####) ```sh reopen```
Reopen the last opened file or folder from your history.

```sh
nuts ntexup reopen
```

####) ```sh documentation```
Open the official NTexUp documentation and tutorial slides.

```sh
nuts ntexup documentation
```

---

###) Project Creation

####) ```sh new [path] [options]```sh
Create a new NTexUp project from a template.

```sh
# Create a new project (interactive template selection)
nuts ntexup new myproject

# Create with a specific template
nuts ntexup new myproject --template slides-basic

# Create and immediately open in viewer
nuts ntexup new myproject --show

# Create and generate PDF
nuts ntexup new myproject --pdf

# Create and generate PDF with custom output
nuts ntexup new myproject --pdf=output.pdf
```

###Options:###
- ```sh --template=<url> | -t <url>``` : Specify template URL or ID
- ```sh --show``` : Open the project in the viewer after creation
- ```sh --pdf[=path]``` : Generate PDF immediately after creation
- ```sh --documentation-pdf[=path]``` : Generate documentation PDF
- ```sh --documentation``` : Open documentation in viewer

---

###) PDF Generation

####) ```sh pdf [path] [options]```
Create a new NTexUp project from a template. If no template is specified, presents an interactive menu of available templates.

```sh
# Create a new project with interactive template selection
nuts ntexup new myproject

# Create with a specific template by ID
nuts ntexup new myproject --template classic-medium

# Create with a specific template by number (from list)
nuts ntexup new myproject --template 5

# Create with a template URL
nuts ntexup new myproject --template https://github.com/user/template.git

# Create and immediately open in viewer
nuts ntexup new myproject --show

# Create and generate PDF
nuts ntexup new myproject --pdf

# Create and generate PDF with custom output
nuts ntexup new myproject --pdf=output.pdf
```

Interactive Template Selection:
When you run nuts ntexup new myproject without specifying a template, you'll see a menu like:

```
Enter template url. You can choose from the following :
[#1 ]   dev:ibtihel-small#1.0     : /path/to/template
[#2 ] * dev:ibtihel-medium#1.0    : /path/to/template
[#3 ]   dev:ibtihel-large#1.0     : /path/to/template
[#4 ]   dev:classic-small#1.0     : /path/to/template
[#5 ] * dev:classic-medium#1.0    : /path/to/template
...
 ? :
```

Templates marked with (*) are recommended for general use. Enter:
- A number (e.g., 5) to select by position
- A template ID (e.g., classic-medium)
- A full template URL

Project Structure:
After creation, a typical project looks like:

```
myproject/
├── main.ntx              # Main project file
└── pages/                # Individual page files
    ├── 0001-plan.ntx     # Introduction/plan
    ├── 0010-slide.ntx    # Content slides
    └── 9901-conclusion.ntx # Conclusion
```

###Options:###
- ```sh --template=<url> | -t <url>``` : Specify template URL, ID, or number
- ```sh --show``` : Open the project in the viewer after creation
- ```sh --pdf[=path]``` : Generate PDF immediately after creation
- ```sh --documentation-pdf[=path]``` : Generate documentation PDF
- ```sh --documentation``` : Open documentation in viewer


####) ```sh documentation-pdf|pdf [options]```
Generate PDF from the official NTexUp documentation.

```sh
# Generate PDF from current directory
nuts ntexup pdf

# Generate PDF from specific document
nuts ntexup pdf document.ntx

# Generate PDF from specific project
nuts ntexup pdf myproject/

# Generate with custom output path
nuts ntexup pdf document.ntx --output=output.pdf

# Generate from multiple documents
nuts ntexup pdf doc1.ntx doc2.ntx --output=./pdfs/
```

Output Behavior:

If --output specifies a directory, PDF names are derived from source files
If --output specifies a file, that exact filename is used (single source only)
Default output is the current directory with .pdf extension

###Options:###
- ```sh --output=<path>``` : Specify output file or directory
- ```sh --var-<name>=<value>``` : Pass custom variables to the document template
- ```sh --dump``` : Show debug information during generation

---

### Templates & Repository

#### ```sh list-templates```
Display all available project templates with their IDs and URLs.

```sh
nuts ntexup list-templates
```

Output shows:
- Template ID
- Template name
- Recommended templates (marked with *)
- Template URL

####) ```sh build-repo  [path]```
Build or update a template repository index. Used for maintaining template collections.

```sh
# Build repository in current directory
nuts ntexup build-repo

# Build repository at specific path
nuts ntexup build-repo ./templates/

# Build with debug output
nuts ntexup build-repo --dump
```

---

###) Editor Integration

####) ```sh install-editor-syntax [editors] [options]```
Install syntax highlighting support for NTexUp (.ntx) files in various text editors.

```sh
# Install for all supported editors
nuts ntexup install-editor-syntax all

# Install for specific editors
nuts ntexup install-editor-syntax vscode intellij

# Install with force overwrite
nuts ntexup install-editor-syntax vim --force
```

###Supported Editors:###
- ##:bold all## : Install for all supported editors
- ##:bold vscode## : Visual Studio Code
- ##:bold intellij## : IntelliJ IDEA
- ##:bold vim## : Vim/NeoVim
- ##:bold gedit## : GNOME Text Editor
- ##:bold kate## : Kate Editor
- ##:bold notepad## +`: Notepad++
- ##:bold jedit## : jEdit

####Options:####
- ```sh --force | -f``` : Overwrite existing syntax files

---

###) Debugging & Utilities

####) ```sh dump```
Display detailed debug information about the current operation or document structure.

```sh
# Show debug info for any command
nuts ntexup pdf document.ntx --dump
nuts ntexup new project --dump
nuts ntexup build-repo --dump
```

####) ```sh --gui** | **--!gui```
Force GUI mode on or off, overriding the default behavior.

```sh
# Force GUI mode
nuts ntexup show document.ntx --gui

# Force terminal mode
nuts ntexup pdf document.ntx --no-gui
```

---

##) COMMON WORKFLOWS

###) Creating Your First Presentation

```nsh
# Create a new presentation project
nuts ntexup new my-presentation

# When prompted, select a template (e.g., enter "5" for classic-medium)
# The project structure is created automatically

# Navigate to your project
cd my-presentation

# Edit your content (main.ntx and files in pages/)
# Use your preferred editor

# Preview in the viewer (opens current directory)
nuts ntexup show

# Generate PDF when ready (uses current directory)
nuts ntexup pdf --output=presentation.pdf
```


###) Working with Existing Projects

```
# Navigate to your project directory
cd myproject

# Open in viewer (uses current directory)
nuts ntexup show

# Or open from any location
nuts ntexup show ~/Documents/myproject

# Reopen last project
nuts ntexup reopen

# Generate PDF from current directory
cd myproject
nuts ntexup pdf
```

###) Creating and Viewing a Presentation

```sh
# Create a new presentation project
nuts ntexup new lecture01 --template slides-modern

# Open it in the viewer
nuts ntexup show lecture01

# Generate PDF when ready
nuts ntexup pdf lecture01 --output=lecture01.pdf
```

###) Batch PDF Generation

```sh
# Generate PDFs for all documents in a folder
nuts ntexup pdf ./lectures/ --output=./pdfs/

# Generate with custom variables
nuts ntexup pdf course.ntx \
  --var-semester="Spring 2025" \
  --var-instructor="Dr. Smith" \
  --output=course-spring-2025.pdf
```

###) Setting Up Your Editor

```sh
# View available templates
nuts ntexup list-templates

# Install syntax highlighting for your editor
nuts ntexup install-editor-syntax vscode

# Or install for all editors at once
nuts ntexup install-editor-syntax all --force

# View documentation
nuts ntexup documentation
```

###) Template Repository Management

```sh
# Create your own template repository
mkdir my-templates
cd my-templates
# (add your template folders)
nuts ntexup build-repo

# Use your custom template
nuts ntexup new myproject --template /path/to/my-templates/my-template
```

---

##) PATH ARGUMENTS

Most commands accept path arguments that can be:
- ##:+:Absolute paths## : `/home/user/documents/file.ntx`
- ##:+:Relative paths## : `./myproject/main.ntx`
- ##:+:Current directory## : `.` or omitted for commands that support it
- ##:+:Git repositories## : `https://github.com/user/repo.git`
- ##:+:Template IDs## : Recognized template identifiers from `list-templates`

---

##) GLOBAL OPTIONS

These options can be used with any command:

- ```sh --gui``` : Force graphical user interface mode
- ```sh --no-gui``` : Force command-line/terminal mode
- ```sh --dump``` : Enable debug output and detailed logging

---

##) EXAMPLES

```sh
# Example 1: Complete workflow for a new document
nuts ntexup new thesis --template academic-paper
nuts ntexup show thesis
# (edit your document)
nuts ntexup pdf thesis --output=thesis-final.pdf

# Example 2: Quick PDF generation
nuts ntexup pdf presentation.ntx --output=slides.pdf

# Example 3: Working with templates
nuts ntexup list-templates
nuts ntexup new project --template slides-minimal --show

# Example 4: Documentation workflow
nuts ntexup documentation
nuts ntexup documentation-pdf --output=ntexup-manual.pdf

# Example 5: Editor setup
nuts ntexup install-editor-syntax vscode vim --force

# Example 6: Repository management
cd ~/ntexup-templates
nuts ntexup build-repo
```

---

##) GETTING HELP

- ##:bold Documentation## : Run `nuts ntexup documentation` to view the full tutorial
- ##:bold Templates## : Run `nuts ntexup list-templates` to see all available templates
- ##:bold Debug## : Add `--dump` to any command for detailed diagnostic output
- ##:bold Source## : Visit https://github.com/thevpc/ntexup for source code and issues

---

##) NOTES

- The viewer automatically detects changes and can reload documents
- Templates marked with (*) in ```sh list-templates``` are recommended for beginners
- Custom variables (```sh --var-*```) are passed to the document template engine
- Syntax highlighting installation requires appropriate editor installation paths
- PDF generation preserves all mathematical notation and formatting
